    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Under non-isotropic remote stress states, the path of a propagating fracture is insensitive to small scale deviations, as around grain boundaries and other heterogeneities in rock. Linear elastic experimental and numerical methods are therefore capable of producing and explaining realistic fracture geometries in rock. Our experimental and numerical modeling gives the unexpected result that open fractures in rock need not intersect at right angles.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
